<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="prep-10-15">
  <title>Resources for 10/15</title>

  <objectives>
    <introduction>
      <p>For the content of this class, you will be expected to be able to do the following:</p>
    </introduction>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          Explain the definition of a <em>rational number</em>.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Prove that a given number is irrational.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Intuitively interpret a real number as a <em>displacement</em> or a point on the number line.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </objectives>

  <subsection xml:id="video-activity-lim-inf-sup">
    <title>Video &amp; Activities for Limit Inferior &amp; Limit Superior</title>
    <p>
      Below are links to a video on <em>limit inferior &amp; limit superior</em>, which is available both on Google Drive and YouTube. It is followed by a link to a set of activities that we will be working on in class. Please watch the video and do as many of the activities as you can before class.
    </p>

    <video youtube="G44ntA-phnY" />

    <example>
      <title>Limit Inferior &amp; Limit Superior</title>
      <p>
        For a sequence <m>\{ x_n \}</m>, recall <m>S_n = \{ x_n,x_{n+1}, \ldots \}</m>, <m>a_n =</m> inf<m>(S_n)</m>,  <m>b_n =</m> sup<m>(S_n)</m>, <m>\displaystyle a_n \rightarrow \lim_{n \rightarrow \infty} \inf x_n</m>, and <m>\displaystyle b_n \rightarrow \lim_{n \rightarrow \infty} \sup x_n</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              Find the limit inferior and limit superior of the following sequences.
            <ol>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>\left\{ (-1)^n \left( 1-\frac{1}{n} \right) \right\}</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>\left\{ 2^{n(-1)^n} \right\}</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>\left\{ 2^{n(-1)^n}-3^{n(-1)^{n+1}} \right\}</m>
                </p>
              </li>
            </ol>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Prove that for each <m>n \in \mathbb{N}</m>, <m>a_n \leq x_n \leq b_n</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Use the Squeeze Theorem to prove that if <m>\displaystyle \lim_{n \rightarrow \infty} \inf x_n = \lim_{n \rightarrow \infty} \sup x_n</m>, then <m>\{ x_n \}</m> converges.  To what number does <m>\{ x_n \}</m> converge?
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              If <m>\{ x_n \}</m> converges, prove it converges to <m>\displaystyle \lim_{n \rightarrow \infty} \inf x_n</m>. Hint: Use the fact that <m>\{ x_n \}</m> is a Cauchy sequence.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              If <m>\{ x_n \}</m> converges, prove it converges to <m>\displaystyle \lim_{n \rightarrow \infty} \sup x_n</m>.
            </p>
          </li>

  <li>
            <p>
              If <m>\{ x_n \}</m> converges, what can you say about <m>\displaystyle \lim_{n \rightarrow \infty} \inf x_n</m> and <m>\displaystyle \lim_{n \rightarrow \infty} \sup x_n</m>?
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </example>
    <p>
      Feel free to post any questions you have on the <url href="https://www.figma.com/board/aJvcTlf1GEiG9CPhI3Vh3h/Notes-for-8-31--Class-Introduction----408--First-Day-Template-?t=ZdQWknSkCn3PfyKQ-6">FigJam board</url> for the next class.
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="video-activity-rep-real">
    <title>Video &amp; Activities for Representing Real Numbers with Cauchy Sequences</title>
    <p>
      Below are links to a video on <em>representing real numbers with Cauchy sequences</em>, which is available both on Google Drive and YouTube. It is followed by a link to a set of activities that we will be working on in class. Please watch the video and do as many of the activities as you can before class.
    </p>

    <video youtube="G44ntA-phnY" />

    <example>
      <title>Representing Real Numbers</title>
      <p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              For each of the real numbers <m>47</m> and <m>\frac29</m>, find three Cauchy sequences of rational numbers that represent the number.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              If <m>\{ a_n \}</m> is a sequence of rational numbers that represents the real number <m>r</m>, find two other rational sequences that represent <m>r</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              For each of <m>\pi</m> and <m>e</m>, use your calculator to find the first eight terms of a Cauchy sequence of rational numbers representing the given real number.  Note: If you are willing to look back at your Calculus 2 experience, you may be able to find an entire sequence that represents <m>e</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              For each real number <m>r</m>, use your experience in #3 to describe how you could construct a Cauchy sequence of rational numbers representing <m>r</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Describe a test for whether or not the rational Cauchy sequences <m>\{ a_n \}</m> and <m>\{ b_n \}</m> represent the same real number.  This test should be understandable to someone who doesn't know what irrational numbers are (but does know all about sequences).
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </example>
    <p>
      Feel free to post any questions you have on the <url href="https://www.figma.com/board/aJvcTlf1GEiG9CPhI3Vh3h/Notes-for-8-31--Class-Introduction----408--First-Day-Template-?t=ZdQWknSkCn3PfyKQ-6">FigJam board</url> for the next class.
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection>
    <title>Optional Reading</title>
    <p>
      Section 2.13: pp. 87-93
    </p>
    <p>
        Representing Real Numbers with Cauchy Sequences
    </p>
  </subsection>
</section>